<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indochina</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as French Indochina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Indochina, or French Indochina, was the federation of French colonial territories in South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a> comprising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>'s three regions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>. It was governed by a governor-general from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>, culminating at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dien-bien-phu">Dien Bien Phu</a> in 1954, was a major stage in the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indochina.jpg" alt="Indochina" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Artanisen · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mainland_Southeast_Asia_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Across the empire, the doctrine of mise en valeur directed colonial economies towards producing raw materials for French industry: rubber from <b>Indochina</b>, phosphates from North Africa, groundnuts from Senegal, cotton from the Sahel.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Paul Doumer, who built <b>Indochina</b>’s federal government and the Hanoi bridge first named after him, later became President of France and was assassinated in 1932.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Thereafter the emperors were chosen and controlled by France, and from 1887 their lands formed part of <b>French Indochina</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nguyen-dynasty">Nguyen dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>This was owed partly to its position as a buffer between British Burma and Malaya on one side and <b>French Indochina</b> on the other, and partly to the diplomacy of Mongkut and Chulalongkorn and their timely concessions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Damas published Poètes d’expression française 1900–1945 in 1946, with an introduction meant as a manifesto; he saw his message as one for the colonised in general and included poets from <b>Indochina</b> and Madagascar.</blockquote><a href="/movement/negritude">Négritude · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>France fought long wars in <b>Indochina</b> and Algeria, Britain against the Mau Mau rising in Kenya, and Portugal held its African colonies until the Carnation Revolution of 1974, after which Mozambique and Angola became independent in 1975.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
